;;; A query's `:goal` splits a name that starts with `neq` — M1e2 **W22**,
;;; register row `W22/kw-neq-in-goal` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1e2.2.22 (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's F1, on the
;;; user's ruling of that day (Q-M1e2.16).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts, `(p a)` and `(neqw a)`; an hrule, `h`, proposing `(tag ?x)` for
;;; each `(p ?x)`; and a query, `(neqw ?y)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `00_ebnf.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `neqw` is a name like any other. R is ∅ and
;;; {(tag a)}, and the query asks about `neqw`, which no hrule proposes: the
;;; read-out names it `outside` the universe, and the query's one class is `?y
;;; = a`. It is the answer of `kw-neq-in-goal-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.22 the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read the goal `(neqw ?y)` as the
;;; guard `(neq w ?y)`. R was right, but the query printed as `(neq w ?y)`,
;;; *the goal holds: it has no variable*, and the read-out named nothing
;;; `outside` the universe. (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and
;;; debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same.) Since then a keyword is a
;;; whole word, at the boundary `00_ebnf.md` § 1 states (the user's ruling of
;;; 2026-11-05 on Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation neqw Node :why "neqw holds of {?1}") (relation tag Node :why "{?1} is tagged")
(p a) (neqw a)
(hrule h () :match (p ?x) :assert (tag ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}; guess that {?x} is tagged")
(query :goal (neqw ?y))
